ented and  post release  this is after they've been reviewed  about  40 percent of modules may be defect-free  that meaning the majority of them still  have defects peer reviews can catch  about 60 of the defects and discipline  disciplined personal practices can  reduce defect rates by up to 75 percent  so what are some of those practices well  the first that  you know that i think everybody should  be using and most of you are probably  familiar with is version control  if you have programmed without using  version control you're probably  accustomed to seeing file names like  this  where you basically have embedded the  version control in the file name  version control you know using tools  like git and github provides a  a much better way to to track  what's been done with code it also  provides a really nice  and and easy way to experiment using  branches  and so if you're not familiar with git i  would suggest maybe starting with  the um the software carpentry uh modules  on  on git and github which are really nice  um another thing that i think gets less  you know many people are familiar with  version control i think many few people  or many fewer people are familiar with  coding style um  and coding style is really important  because you know as the founder of  python guida van rossum said  code is read much more often than it's  written  so readability counts um now nearly  every language has a language specific  convention for coding style  and following those conventions makes  your code easier for others to read and  understand and it's important to  keep in mind that others includes your  future self right when you look back at  code six months from now  it's as if you are a different person  and so if the code is readable you'll be  you'll be much more easily able to  figure out what on earth was i  trying to do there now there's also  a lot of sort of language independent  principles of software design i'm  showing a few of the books here that  that 